@@s001 How to answer at the oral
@@s002 <span>Exam</span>
@@s003 How to answer at the oral
@@s004 The course questions always come back in the same 6 types. For each one: the outline of the answer and a model sentence in English, to adapt. They hold for the written answers of the assignments, for the oral feedback and for the exam.
@@s005 The general outline (without preparation)
@@s006 Talking for 3 minutes on a question
@@s007 <mark class="dato">1. define</mark> the terms of the question
@@s008 "A data race occurs when…". The course definitions, word for word: <a href="../teoria/basi-glossario.html">glossary</a>.
@@s009 <mark class="ora">2. small example</mark>: the counter with two threads
@@s010 <code>count++</code> with two threads covers race condition, lock, interleaving, data race and volatile.
@@s011 <mark class="due">3. the mechanism</mark>: interleaving or happens-before
@@s012 Write or draw the sequence <code>t1(1), t2(1), …</code> or the chain of HB pairs.
@@s013 "Therefore…". Answer yes/no when the question is yes/no.
@@s014 <mark class="no">5. the limit</mark>
@@s015 What the solution does <b>not</b> guarantee (volatile: no mutual exclusion; non-fair lock: starvation possible). It is the point that separates the high-grade answers.
@@s016 Type 1 · "Explain why your solution is correct"
@@s017 Exercises 1.1.4, 1.1.7, 1.2.3, 1.3.2. The text asks to use "critical sections, interleavings, race conditions, mutual exclusion".
@@s018 Outline
@@s019 what is the <mark class="dato">critical section</mark>
@@s020 The lines that read and write the shared state.
@@s021 the lock gives <mark class="ok">mutual exclusion</mark>
@@s022 At most one thread in the critical section.
@@s023 Those in which the operations of the two threads overlap.
@@s024 so <mark class="ok">the only possible output</mark> is…
@@s025 And why no others.
@@s026 Model sentence
@@s027 "The critical section is the read and write of <i>count</i> in <i>increment()</i>. We protect it with a ReentrantLock, calling <i>lock()</i> before it and <i>unlock()</i> in a finally block. The lock guarantees mutual exclusion: at most one thread executes the critical section at a time. Therefore no interleaving can place a read of one thread between the read and the write of the other, so no increment is lost. Each of the 20 million increments reads the value written by the previous one, and the only possible output is 20,000,000."
@@s028 Type 2 · "Is this output possible?"
@@s029 Exercises 1.2.2, 1.2.4, the minimum value of the counter.
@@s030 <b>If yes</b>: write a concrete interleaving, action by action, and show that it produces that output. One example is enough.
@@s031 <b>If no</b>: by contradiction. "Assume an interleaving produces it. Then … must happen before …, but … Contradiction." Here one example is not enough: you must cover <b>all</b> the interleavings.
@@s032 Model sentence
@@s033 "Yes. Consider the interleaving t1(1), t2(1), t1(2), t2(2): both threads read <i>count</i> = 0 before either writes, so both write 1 and one increment is lost. Hence the program can print 1."
@@s034 Type 3 · "Show, using the Java memory model, that…"
@@s035 Exercises 3.1–3.3. Here one does not speak of interleavings but of <b>happens-before</b>.
@@s036 The strategy in 6 steps
@@s037 1. number the operations and <mark class="dato">classify the actions</mark>
@@s038 variable access, synchronization, other.
@@s039 2. <mark class="dato">program order</mark> of each thread
@@s040 The same in all executions.
@@s041 If there are many, group them by "who takes the lock first".
@@s042 One per family of synchronization order.
@@s043 5. <mark class="due">conflicting pairs</mark>: same non-volatile variable, at least one write
@@s044 Only between different threads.
@@s045 6. is every pair ordered in every HB? <mark class="ok">yes → no data race</mark> · <mark class="no">no → data race</mark>
@@s046 For "yes" show the path of arrows; for "no" show that it does not exist. <a href="../teoria/l03-esercizio-31.html">Exercise 3.1 worked out</a>.
@@s047 Model sentence
@@s048 "t1(2) and t2(2) are conflicting actions: both write the non-volatile variable <i>count</i>. The only pairs relating t1 and t2 come from the thread start and termination rules, which relate them to the main thread only. Hence neither t1(2) → t2(2) nor t2(2) → t1(2) is in the happens-before order, in any execution, and the program contains a data race."
@@s049 Type 4 · "Is this class thread-safe?"
@@s050 Exercises 4.1.2, 4.2.2. The slides: "you must always cover these elements".
@@s051 Outline in English
@@s052 "<b>Class state</b>: the fields are … <b>Escaping</b>: all fields are private and no method returns a reference to them, so the state does not escape. <b>Safe publication</b>: … is final / has its default value / is volatile, so its initialization happens-before any access. <b>Immutability</b>: … never changes after construction. <b>Mutual exclusion</b>: every access to the mutable fields … holds the lock …, so all conflicting accesses are ordered by the monitor rule. Therefore there are no data races on the class state, and the class is thread-safe."
@@s053 If the class is <b>not</b> thread-safe: indicate the element that is missing and the two conflicting actions that are not ordered. <a href="../teoria/l04-thread-safety.html">Thread-safe classes</a>.
@@s054 Type 5 · "Can the program loop forever?" (visibility)
@@s055 Exercises 2.2, 3.3. The answer is almost always "yes, it is <b>possible</b>", even if when you run it you do not see it.
@@s056 Model sentence
@@s057 "Yes, it is possible. The write <i>x = 42</i> in main and the reads of <i>x</i> in t1 are not ordered by happens-before: there is no lock, no volatile variable and no join relating them. Therefore the Java memory model does not guarantee that t1 ever sees the new value, and the loop may run forever. Declaring <i>x</i> volatile fixes it: by the volatile rule, the write happens-before every subsequent read."
@@s058 Type 6 · "Is it fair? Can a thread starve?"
@@s059 Exercises 2.1.2, 2.1.4, 4.1.4.
@@s060 Define: <b>starvation</b> = a thread that wants to enter never manages to, while others keep entering.
@@s061 Show the scenario: a continuous flow of readers that never lets the count drop to 0.
@@s062 Distinguish: the <b>solution</b> (for example the <code>writer</code> flag that blocks new readers) from the <b>guarantee of the lock</b> (non-fair lock: the choice of who enters is arbitrary). <a href="../teoria/l02-fairness.html">Fairness</a>.
@@s063 Words not to mix up
@@s064 Do not say
@@s065 When you mean
@@s066 Say instead
@@s067 race condition
@@s068 two conflicting accesses not ordered by HB
@@s069 <b>data race</b>
@@s070 data race
@@s071 the result depends on the order (count = 1)
@@s072 <b>race condition</b>
@@s073 synchronization action
@@s074 read or write of a variable, even volatile
@@s075 <b>variable access</b>
@@s076 "the lock makes it atomic"
@@s077 no other thread can execute the critical section at the same time
@@s078 <b>mutual exclusion</b> on the critical section
@@s079 "it works, I ran it"
@@s080 the program is correct
@@s081 an argument over <b>all</b> the interleavings / all the executions
@@s082 thread-safe (in a vague sense)
@@s083 no data race on the fields
@@s084 <b>thread-safe</b> according to the course definition
